WEBVTT

00:00:00.469 --> 00:00:03.760
A farmer with a harvest to sell has one fear:

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:05.320
tomorrow's price.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.300
So he wants a promise:

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:11.080
a buyer who pays an agreed price on a set date,

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.800
whatever the market does.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:13.900
In Chicago,

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.040
those promises were written into contracts,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.600
and soon the contracts themselves were traded.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.600
Then, in 1973,

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.980
Chicago opened an exchange for options.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:23.860
With a put,

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.034
the farmer buys the right

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:26.820
to sell at the agreed price,

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.420
but not the obligation.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.740
If prices fall, he's protected.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.860
If they rise, he sells at the market.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.300
Over the decades, the pits became screens,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.680
and the screens became phones.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.480
But the rules stayed inside private systems:

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:42.840
who took your trade,

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.400
what backs it, how the price was set.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.220
On-chain, those rules become public code.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.480
You keep your keys,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.000
the collateral is visible,

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.640
and the market never sleeps.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:55.860
That's why we build on NEAR Protocol,

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.426
where smart contracts hold the rules

00:00:57.416 --> 00:01:00.080
and settle every trade in the open.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.477
Nightshade sharding is designed

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:02.900
to scale with the market,

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.860
and you sign every trade from your own wallet.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.040
For private intents,

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.800
we're exploring trusted execution environments,

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.820
with trades still settling on NEAR.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.060
On that foundation,

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.000
we built Optionality:

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.100
a pool-led options market.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.752
Order books need a matching engine

00:01:19.742 --> 00:01:21.740
and a trader on the other side,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.260
and thin books make orders slip.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.020
In Optionality, the pool is the other side.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:28.640
It quotes every spread,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.000
so you trade at the price you confirm.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.240
Choose a spread, review the quote, confirm.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.040
Your risk is fixed at entry,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.040
and nobody gets liquidated.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.080
Before accepting,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.480
the pool reserves the ticket's maximum payout,

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.380
so every winning ticket is already covered.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.360
Then exit at the pool's quote,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.180
or hold to expiry:

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:49.980
no venue entry fee,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.920
no early exit.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.000
Holding has a second upside.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.520
Many options expire worthless, but here,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.500
a loss can still win.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.677
Every position held to expiry enters

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:02.920
our planned daily Loss Lottery:

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.920
losing premiums fill the prize,

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:08.880
and once a day, one participant takes the pot.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.780
Every position lives on a chart:

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.220
the price over time,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.840
and at each expiry a wall,

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.640
green for calls, red for puts.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:22.240
Each shows its P&L and time left. Tap a wall,

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.960
choose put or call and an amount,

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.700
and the pool quotes it.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.440
Next, we're building private copy trading:

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.180
traders publish their P&L,

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.700
and only subscribers see their trades,

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.620
never their wallet.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.060
Finally, at expiry,

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.540
no single source decides the price.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.540
Our exchange index, Kraken,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.640
OKX and Pyth each sign their own price,

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.008
and the contract settles only

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:48.280
when independent sources agree.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.980
Optionality. On-chain options,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.381
powered by NEAR, now live on testnet.
